{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d418b9c6fe663009","name":"card_event_colossus","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_colossus","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"COLOSSUS","text":"Copy three random orders in the enemy deck and shuffle them into yours. They get: \"Draw a card.\"","flavorText":"Colossus was a secret British codebreaking computer built for Bletchley Park during WWII. It was designed to attack the Lorenz cipher, a German high level teleprinter system used for messages between the German High Command and army commands in occupied Europe. Unlike the better known Bombe, which helped against Enigma, Colossus worked on the “Tunny” traffic produced by Lorenz machines. The design came from the work of Max Newman’s section and was built by General Post Office engineer Tommy Flowers with his team at Dollis Hill. The first Colossus was operational in December 1943 and reached Bletchley Park in early 1944. The faster Mark 2 first ran on 1 June 1944, just in time for the Normandy landings. It used thousands of thermionic valves, read punched paper tape at high speed, and tested possible cipher wheel settings through switches and plug panels. By the end of the war, ten Colossus machines were operating. Their existence remained secret for decades, which delayed public recognition of their role in early electronic computing.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_colossus","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_colossus_title","SourceString":"COLOSSUS","LocalizedString":"COLOSSUS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_colossus_text","SourceString":"Copy three random orders in the enemy deck and shuffle them into yours. They get: \"Draw a card.\"","LocalizedString":"Copy three random orders in the enemy deck and shuffle them into yours. They get: \"Draw a card.\""},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_colossus_history","SourceString":"Colossus was a secret British codebreaking computer built for Bletchley Park during WWII. It was designed to attack the Lorenz cipher, a German high level teleprinter system used for messages between the German High Command and army commands in occupied Europe. Unlike the better known Bombe, which helped against Enigma, Colossus worked on the “Tunny” traffic produced by Lorenz machines. The design came from the work of Max Newman’s section and was built by General Post Office engineer Tommy Flowers with his team at Dollis Hill. The first Colossus was operational in December 1943 and reached Bletchley Park in early 1944. The faster Mark 2 first ran on 1 June 1944, just in time for the Normandy landings. It used thousands of thermionic valves, read punched paper tape at high speed, and tested possible cipher wheel settings through switches and plug panels. By the end of the war, ten Colossus machines were operating. Their existence remained secret for decades, which delayed public recognition of their role in early electronic computing.","LocalizedString":"Colossus was a secret British codebreaking computer built for Bletchley Park during WWII. It was designed to attack the Lorenz cipher, a German high level teleprinter system used for messages between the German High Command and army commands in occupied Europe. Unlike the better known Bombe, which helped against Enigma, Colossus worked on the “Tunny” traffic produced by Lorenz machines. The design came from the work of Max Newman’s section and was built by General Post Office engineer Tommy Flowers with his team at Dollis Hill. The first Colossus was operational in December 1943 and reached Bletchley Park in early 1944. The faster Mark 2 first ran on 1 June 1944, just in time for the Normandy landings. It used thousands of thermionic valves, read punched paper tape at high speed, and tested possible cipher wheel settings through switches and plug panels. By the end of the war, ten Colossus machines were operating. Their existence remained secret for decades, which delayed public recognition of their role in early electronic computing."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'For_the_Queen'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/For_the_Queen.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/COLOSSUS.COLOSSUS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_colossus_photo","SourceString":"Colossus 10 in Block H at Bletchley Park in 1945.","LocalizedString":"Colossus 10 in Block H at Bletchley Park in 1945."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"Notes":"Image needed to be customized","isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"possibleOrders","type":"TArray<UBaseCardObject*>","direction":"in"},{"name":"tempCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_colossus","owner":"card_event_colossus_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_LessEqual_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue_1","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_bool_True_if_break_was_hit_Variable","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable_1","type":"int32","direct